[{"id": "R025", "text": "The person presses the bracket against the fastener, tests the loose part with a pencil, and confirms normal readings. They mark the bracket edge with pencil and place the pencil under the fastener, keeping it there in case the fastener remains loose."}, {"id": "R026", "text": "The person presses the fastener against the bracket and checks the sensor cable, then moves the bracket and checks the cable again. After apologizing, they switch from touching the sensor to photographing it."}, {"id": "R027", "text": "The person places the bracket on the step, listens, observes the loose part move, and replaces the fastener. They continue watching the rig while the bracket remains loose and the readings stay normal."}, {"id": "R028", "text": "The person presses the fastener against the bracket until the loose bolt catches, moves the bracket closer, and taps the fastener twice. They state that it is not loose but not normal, and leave the bolt loose enough for later measurement."}, {"id": "R029", "text": "The person watches the fastener move, sets it on the bench, and tests the bracket again. They plan to stop and work on the fastener if it moves, or mark and retain the bracket if the sensor feels loose."}, {"id": "R030", "text": "The person tightens the bracket with a thumb, marks the loose side with chalk, tests the bracket again, and marks it again. They pause, then tighten the bracket once more."}, {"id": "R031", "text": "The person presses and taps the bracket with a wrench while monitoring a ticking sound and a light. After repeated checks, they remove the fastener, causing the ticking to resume while the bracket remains in place."}, {"id": "R032", "text": "The person removes the fastener, presses the loose bracket down, and gets an incorrect reading. They check the ruler, replace the incorrect one, observe that the bracket is no longer loose, and keep the fastener."}, {"id": "R033", "text": "The person presses the fastener into the ground with a thumbnail, causing the loose part to catch and shift, and keeps the bracket beside the rig. They leave the sensor intact and sufficiently loose to measure the axle and gear shifts, possibly to reveal a hidden spring."}, {"id": "R034", "text": "The person presses the fastener down, checks the bracket position, and moves it slightly after it feels almost cracked. Because the readings remain normal, they keep watching the bracket, replace the fastener, and wait."}, {"id": "R035", "text": "The person places the fastener under the loose bracket, leaves the sensor loose, and confirms that readings return. They stop using the loose fastener, leave it in one place, and leave the sensor where it is."}, {"id": "R036", "text": "The person presses the bracket down, taps the page edge twice, states that it is fine, and holds the fastener against the rig. When the loose fastener catches the page edge, they tap the bracket with a fingertip instead of a nail, and the fastener slides free beside the sensor."}]
